/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Filters Streaming filters
 * @brief Two generic SISO ProcessorBase implementations, configured by coefficients.
 *
 * Include `<DspCore_Filters.h>` or FIR.h/IIR.h. Coefficient calculators are
 * functions in @ref DspCore_Filters_Biquad, not processing classes.
 * Process advances one complete input block with processor-owned persistent history.
 * Both filters also implement @ref DspCore::Processing::ProcessorBase with one
 * input and one output port: block processing advances the same state once per
 * input value and produces the same number of output values. No processing,
 * Process and Reset do not allocate, schedule, clip or throw. Configure may
 * resize processor-owned CPSTL storage.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Filters_Contract Processing contracts
 *
 * - **SPEC-FILT-1**: Float/double samples, coefficients and computed state must
 *   be finite. Invalid configuration/update leaves coefficients, history and
 *   output unchanged. A successful Configure resets delays. Scalar input and
 *   output may alias. Raw coefficient array extent is a caller precondition.
 * - **SPEC-FILT-2**: FIR<T> owns numerator coefficients and input history in
 *   cpstd::vector storage. Configure accepts any positive finite runtime tap
 *   count, reallocates only there, resets history and makes that count
 *   observable through Taps(). b[0] multiplies the current input. Default
 *   configuration is one-tap identity. Equation:
 *   y[n]=sum(b[k]*x[n-k]).
 * - **SPEC-FILT-3**: IIR<T> owns numerator, denominator and their delay
 *   histories in cpstd::vector storage. Configure accepts independently sized,
 *   positive runtime counts through raw pointers/counts, array references, or
 *   a struct exposing numerator and denominator arrays. All values must be
 *   finite and a0 nonzero. Normalize every coefficient by a0 and validate the
 *   result before committing. Negative a0 is supported. Source arrays/structs
 *   are unchanged.
 * - **SPEC-FILT-4**: IIR uses the generic direct-form I recurrence:
 *   y[n]=sum(b[k]/a0*x[n-k])-sum(a[k]/a0*y[n-k], k>=1).
 *   Default configuration is identity. History and normalized coefficients are
 *   processor-owned; copying a filter duplicates state. Configure resets history,
 *   may resize storage and may cause transients. No automatic coefficient
 *   smoothing is performed.
 * - **SPEC-FILT-5**: The ProcessorBase block overload requires exactly one
 *   valid finite input and one output with capacity for the complete input
 *   block. Invalid views and non-finite input are rejected before processing.
 *   On a finite arithmetic failure, OutputBlock::size reports the valid prefix
 *   already committed. Successful blocks have one output per input and no
 *   added windowing latency.
 *
 * Generic IIR validates representability, not arbitrary-order pole stability.
 * Callers supplying custom coefficients own their stability decisions. Biquad
 * calculators check strict second-order stability before returning a design.
 * A finite configuration does not guarantee finite results for every input;
 * overflow rejects that sample without consuming it or clipping it.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * auto coefficients = DspCore::Filters::Biquad::LowPass(48000.f,1000.f,0.707f);
 * DspCore::Filters::IIR<float> filter;
 * if (!filter.Configure(coefficients)) { return; }
 * const float input[]={1.f,0.f};
 * float output[2]={};
 * DspCore::Processing::InputBlock<float> inputs(input,2);
 * DspCore::Processing::OutputBlock<float> outputs(output,2);
 * bool ok=filter.Process(&inputs,1,&outputs,1) && outputs.size==2;
 * @endcode
 *
 * Configure IIR with other coefficient counts for higher/lower-order recurrences;
 * the processor does not depend on the Biquad header or a particular calculator.
 * Examples: Filters/FIR, Filters/IIR and Filters/Biquad.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Filters_Biquad Biquad coefficient calculators
 * @ingroup DspCore_Filters
 * @brief Free functions returning six-coefficient second-order descriptions.
 *
 * Namespace DspCore::Filters::Biquad contains Coefficients<T>, LowPass,
 * HighPass and IsStable. There is no Biquad processing class or Design
 * namespace. Coefficients<T> contains only numerator[3] (b0,b1,b2) and
 * denominator[3] (a0,a1,a2), with identity defaults. It is trivially copyable
 * and occupies six scalars. One value can configure independent IIR instances.
 *
 * LowPass(sampleRate,cutoff,Q) and HighPass(...) return this struct by value,
 * including raw a0; IIR performs normalization. Require finite positive
 * sample rate/Q and 0<cutoff<sampleRate/2. Invalid or numerically unrepresentable
 * designs return denominator[0]==0 so IIR::Configure rejects them, preserving
 * its previous configuration/history. No exception or hidden identity fallback.
 *
 * Calculations follow the [W3C Audio EQ Cookbook](https://www.w3.org/TR/audio-eq-cookbook/)
 * (Robert Bristow-Johnson), with half-angle numerator identities. IsStable
 * validates finite/normalizable coefficients and strict normalized pole bounds:
 * -1<a2/a0<1 and -(1+a2/a0)<a1/a0<(1+a2/a0). Boundary poles are rejected.
 * This optional check belongs to the coefficient calculation, not the generic
 * IIR recurrence. No hidden cutoff/Q clamps or processing state are involved.
 */
/** @namespace DspCore::Filters
 * @brief Generic FIR/IIR processing and coefficient calculation namespaces.
 */
/** @namespace DspCore::Filters::Biquad
 * @brief Free second-order coefficient calculators and Coefficients<T> values.
 */
